Sure! To update a break node’s label to `'loopEnd'`, you’ll need to pass the node (as a JSON‑stringified JavaScript object) and the desired label to the `updateBreak` function. Here’s how you can do it:

```javascript
// Example node (replace this with your actual node)
const node = {
  type: "BreakStatement",
  label: "oldLabel",   // Current label
  // ... other properties ...
};

// Call the function
const result = await functions.updateBreak({
  node: JSON.stringify(node),
  label: "loopEnd"
});

console.log(result);
```

If the node’s current label is already `"loopEnd"`, the function will simply return the original node unchanged. Let me know if you’d like help formatting your specific node or if you run into any issues!
